ancy B.; Tanshi, I; Upham, Nathan S.; Willoughby, Anna R; Zijlstra, J. S.; Geiselman, Cullen</span><span class="c9 c31">1</span></p><p class="c4 c8"><span class="c9 c30"></span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c9 c59">1</span><span class="c7">Bat Eco-Interaction Project</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c9 c31">2</span><span class="c7">Department of Zoology and Entomology, University of Pretoria, Pretoria, South Africa</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c9 c31">3</span><span class="c7">Department of Life Sciences, Imperial College London, Ascot, London, UK</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">Plazi</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c9 c59">&nbsp;</span><span class="c7">Departamento de Fitopatologia, Universidade de Bras&iacute;lia, Bras&iacute;lia, DF, Brazil</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">Departamento de Zoologia, Universidade Federal do Paran&aacute;, Curitiba, PR, Brazil</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">College of Veterinary Medicine, University of Minnesota, St. Paul, MN, USA</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">Ronin Institute for Independent Scholarship, Monclair, NJ, USA</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">Center for Biodiversity and Ecological Restoration, UC Santa Barbara, Santa Barbara, CA, USA, </span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">Bucknell University, Department of Biology, Lewisburg, PA, USA</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">Department of Mammalogy, Division of Vertebrate Zoology, American Museum of Natural History, New York, NY 10024</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">Department of Biology, University of Washington, Seattle, USA.</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">Small Mammal Conservation Organization, Benin City, Nigeria.</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">Arizona State University, School of Life Sciences, Tempe, AZ, USA</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">Odum School of Ecology, University of Georgia, GA, USA</span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">Meise Botanic Garden, Meise, Belgium</span></p><p class="c4 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c7">Corresponding author: Aja C. Sherman </span></p><p class="c4"><span class="c9">Email: </span><span class="c9 c43"><a class="c60" href="mailto:aja@batbase.org">aja@batbase.org</a></span></p><p class="c4 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c4 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c4 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><hr style="page-break-before:always;display:none;"><p class="c4 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><h1 class="c1"><span class="c18 c9">Abstract </span></h1><p class="c3"><span class="c9">Species names provide the foundation for organizing biological knowledge, linking information across evolution, conservation, </span><span class="c9">genomics</span><sup><a href="#cmnt1" id="cmnt_ref1">[a]</a></sup><span class="c9">, epidemiology, and biodiversity monitoring. Yet the taxonomic concepts associated with those names often change through time, creating challenges for interpreting and integrating data collected under different classifications. Bats</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;provide a useful model system for examining these challenges because they have undergone extensive taxonomic revision in recent decades and increasingly serve as focal taxa in research ranging from conservation to disease ecology. To address this challenge, we developed the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA), a framework that reconciles taxonomic concepts across </span><span class="c9">fifteen </span><span class="c9">major </span><span class="c9">bat </span><span class="c9">authorities spanning 45 years (1980&ndash;2025). The BTA enables users to track how species </span><span class="c9">concepts</span><sup><a href="#cmnt2" id="cmnt_ref2">[b]</a></sup><span class="c9">&nbsp;have changed through time, linking historical and contemporary classifications while preserving provenance and alternative treatments. Application of the framework revealed substantial disagreement among contemporary taxonomic authorities, with hundreds of conflicting species treatments persisting despite increasing efforts toward standardization. To demonstrate its utility, we used the BTA to audit taxonomic consistency within the IUCN Red List, identifying 287 taxonomic </span><span class="c9">modifications</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;affecting </span><span class="c9">~</span><span class="c9">20% of assessed bat species</span><sup><a href="#cmnt3" id="cmnt_ref3">[c]</a></sup><sup><a href="#cmnt4" id="cmnt_ref4">[d]</a></sup><span class="c9">, </span><span class="c9">and </span><span class="c7">demonstrating how taxonomic discrepancies can propagate into conservation infrastructure. Although developed using bats as a model system, the challenges addressed by the BTA extend across biodiversity science wherever changing taxonomies influence the interpretation of biological data. By making taxonomic concepts explicit, traceable, and interoperable, the framework supports more transparent, reproducible, and integrated use of biological data.</span></p><p class="c4 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c47"><span class="c41">Keywords:</span><span class="c7">&nbsp;Taxonomic concepts; Taxonomic alignment; Biodiversity informatics; Biodiversity data integration; Species concepts</span></p><hr style="page-break-before:always;display:none;"><p class="c25 c14 c8"><span class="c18 c9"></span></p><h1 class="c1"><span class="c18 c9">Introduction </span></h1><p class="c3"><span class="c9">Taxonomy is the primary indexing </span><span class="c9">system</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;of biological knowledge. T</span><span class="c9">axonomic names</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;link data from field observations to museum specimens, </span><span class="c9">genomic sequences to ecological interactions</span><sup><a href="#cmnt5" id="cmnt_ref5">[e]</a></sup><span class="c9">, </span><span class="c9">hosts to pathogen records</span><span class="c9">, and conservation status to legal protections. Across disciplines, species names function as the keys by which data are organized, integrated, and interpreted. Effective research, management, and policy depend on clearly defined taxonomic units that reflect biological reality and can be tracked through space and time (Voigt et al 2016; Lyal et al. 2008; </span><span class="c9">Sharma et al. 2024</span><span class="c9">). In this sense, taxonomy is not merely a classificatory exercise; it is the foundational infrastructure for biodiversity science, public health, and environmental governance </span><span class="c9">(</span><span class="c9 c15">CITE CITE</span><span class="c9">)</span><span class="c7">.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">The global taxonomic infrastructure operates under increasing strain. Global biodiversity is declining at an unprecedented rate (Keck et al. 2025), intensifying the need for accurate species identification and tracking, while biological data are being generated, digitized, and aggregated at unprecedented scales </span><span class="c9">(</span><span class="c9 c54">Bayraktarov</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;et al., 2019</span><sup><a href="#cmnt6" id="cmnt_ref6">[f]</a></sup><span class="c9">; </span><span class="c9">Heberling et al., 2021</span><sup><a href="#cmnt7" id="cmnt_ref7">[g]</a></sup><span class="c9">; Ji et al., 2026; GBIF No. 12, 2025)</span><span class="c7">. Occurrence records, genomic sequences, ecological networks, and host&ndash;pathogen databases &ndash; to give some examples - are increasingly integrated across global repositories such as the Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF), GenBank, BOLD, and institutional collections. The reliability of these systems depends on the assumption that taxonomic names consistently refer to comparable biological entities. Increasingly, that assumption does not hold.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">A central but often overlooked issue is that a </span><span class="c9">name string - the sequence of letters used to represent the name of a biological taxon (e.g., </span><span class="c9 c42">Homo sapiens</span><span class="c9">)</span><sup><a href="#cmnt8" id="cmnt_ref8">[h]</a></sup><span class="c9">&nbsp;- is not equivalent to the taxonomic species concept it represents. Taxonomic names are shorthand for evolving hypotheses about species boundaries and evolutionary relationships (Zachos,</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;2018</span><sup><a href="#cmnt9" id="cmnt_ref9">[i]</a></sup><span class="c9">). The same </span><span class="c9">binomial</span><sup><a href="#cmnt10" id="cmnt_ref10">[j]</a></sup><span class="c9">&nbsp;may refer to different circumscriptions &ndash; the set of populations included in a taxon - across taxonomic classifications </span><span class="c9">or time, while different names may refer to the same lineage.</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;Advances in integrative taxonomy increasingly combine molecular, morphological, ecological, behavioural, and biogeographic evidence to refine species boundaries and classifications (Dayrat, 2005; Padial et al., 2010; Solari et al., 2019). As a result, species concepts are continually reassessed as new evidence emerges (de Queiroz, 2025; Zachos, 2018). Differences in synonymy, rank, genus placement, spelling, or species delimitation therefore reflect scientific progress, yet biodiversity data systems frequently treat names as stable identifiers </span><span class="c9">(</span><span class="c9">Page 2008</span><sup><a href="#cmnt11" id="cmnt_ref11">[k]</a></sup><span class="c9">)</span><sup><a href="#cmnt12" id="cmnt_ref12">[l]</a></sup><span class="c7">. Without explicit tracking of taxonomic species concepts, including who defined them, when, and under what circumscription, data repositories implicitly assume stability. As a result, records may be combined or split based solely on name matching, rather than if they represent the same biological concept.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">Because scientific names underpin biological databases, museum collections, scientific publications, genomic resources, and conservation assessments, changes in species circumscriptions can propagate throughout interconnected biological infrastructures and downstream applications (Figure 1).</span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3"><span style="overflow: hidden; display: inline-block; margin: 0.00px 0.00px; border: 0.00px solid #000000; transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); -webkit-transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); width: 624.00px; height: 380.93px;"><img alt="" src="data:image/png;base64," style="width: 624.00px; height: 380.93px; margin-left: -0.00px; margin-top: -0.00px; transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); -webkit-transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px);" title=""></span></p><p class="c14 c40"><span class="c41">Figure 1: Downstream Effects of Taxonomic Changes: Why taxonomic concept alignment matters</span><span class="c9">. (a) Historically, many species were recognised as single, widespread taxonomic concepts. The bent-winged bat </span><span class="c9 c42">Miniopterus schreibersii</span><span class="c7">&nbsp;is used here as an illustrative example of a species that was once thought to encompass a broad geographic distribution. (b) As scientific knowledge advances, taxonomic revisions can divide historical concepts into multiple species with distinct distributions and biological identities. Although scientific names may persist, the biological entities they represent can change substantially through time. (c) This creates challenges across biological data because historical records and downstream applications are typically linked only by scientific names, making it unclear which contemporary species concept those records actually represent. (d) Taxonomic concept alignment, such as the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA) framework presented here, addresses this challenge by explicitly linking historical and contemporary taxonomic concepts through documented concept relationships and provenance, enabling reproducible integration of biodiversity data across taxonomic authorities, databases, and time periods. Distribution data shown in panel (a) are based on the third edition of Mammal Species of the World (MSW3), whereas panel (b) reflects species concepts recognised by the Mammal Diversity Database version 2 (MDD2).&nbsp; Icons are from FreePic</span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">Despite the centrality of taxonomy to many scientific disciplines, there remains no widely available framework that transparently aligns competing taxo</span><span class="c9">nomic concepts across</span><sup><a href="#cmnt13" id="cmnt_ref13">[m]</a></sup><span class="c7">&nbsp;authorities and time. A range of tools exists to reconcile taxonomic name strings across datasets, including fuzzy matching algorithms (e.g., Taxamatch; Rees, 2014), web-based resolution services (e.g., TNRS; Boyle et al., 2013), multi-database query frameworks (Berendsohn et al. 2026), and phylogeny matching tools (e.g., Phylomatcher; Radar et al., 2023). These approaches are effective for correcting misspellings, resolving orthographic variants, and identifying synonyms, but they operate primarily at the level of name strings rather than dynamic taxonomic concepts (Tsang et al., 2016). Consequently, they do not resolve deeper incongruences arising from alternative species circumscriptions, rank changes, or conflicting taxonomic treatments (Franz et al., 2016; Patterson et al., 2010). Large biodiversity infrastructures such as GBIF maintain extensive synonymy and cross-reference records, yet their effective use still requires manual interpretation to determine which taxonomic concept a name actually represents. </span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">Even in the largest data repositories, significant ingestion gaps persist (Garnett, 2020). When aggregators attempt to harmonize data from hundreds of sources, they frequently adopt a single preferred taxonomic treatment (Mesibov, 2013; 2018; Franz et al., 2016). As demonstrated by Mesibov (2013, 2018), automated look-up tables often prioritize string-to-string matching over taxonomic concepts, potentially overwriting the original taxonomic intent of source datasets. Consequently, records may be reassigned to accepted names that reflect different taxonomic concepts or an outdated circumscription (Burgin et al., 2018). Without provenance or concept-level tracking, it becomes difficult for researchers to determine whether a name reflects a contemporary taxonomic consensus or simply an artifact of an automated harmonization process.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">As taxonomic discovery accelerates and global data integration expands, the need for transparent, versioned alignment of taxonomic concepts becomes increasingly urgent. Currently, no freely available framework provides concept-driven reconciliation of taxonomic names across taxonomic authorities while simultaneously preserving alternative interpretations, historical provenance, and the temporal and geographic context in which taxonomic decisions are made </span><span class="c9 c15">(Radar et al., 2023).</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">Bats (Order Chiroptera) provide an ideal system in which to develop and test a concept alignment framework. The number of recognized bat species has increased by more than one-third since the early 1990s, reflecting rapid advances in phylogenetics, integrative taxonomy, and the resolution of cryptic species complexes (Monadjem et al., 2024; Burgin et al., 2025). As illustrated in Figure 1, taxonomic revisions can substantially alter the biological meaning associated with scientific names, creating challenges for many downstream disciplines, including biodiversity monitoring, conservation assessments, ecological studies, ecosystem service evaluations, and disease surveillance. Although bats are used here as a model system, these challenges extend across biodiversity science wherever data are linked through taxonomic names that change through time.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">Here, we present the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA), an open-access, version-controlled framework designed to document and align taxonomic concepts across major taxonomic authorities spanning more than four decades. Rather than acting as a new taxonomic authority, the BTA preserves and organizes how species concepts are treated across alternative classifications, explicitly recording synonymy, rank changes, genus transfers, and competing interpretations. By shifting the focus from name strings to taxonomic concepts, the BTA provides a transparent bridge among biodiversity repositories, literature, and conservation workflows. Using bats as a globally relevant case study, we demonstrate how concept-level alignment can reconcile alternative taxonomic treatments, quantify disagreement among leading taxonomic authorities, and reveal inconsistencies in conservation infrastructure through an audit of the IUCN Red List. Together, these analyses illustrate the broader value of taxonomic concept alignment</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;for </span><span class="c9">reproducible biodiversity science, conservation</span><span class="c7">, and biodiversity data integration in an era of rapid taxonomic change.</span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><hr style="page-break-before:always;display:none;"><p class="c14 c8 c25"><span class="c18 c9"></span></p><h1 class="c1"><span class="c18 c9">The Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA) Tool</span></h1><p class="c3"><span class="c7">The Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA) was developed through a three-stage workflow comprising the acquisition and digitization of taxonomic authorities, alignment of taxonomic concepts across classifications, and dissemination of the resulting resource as a version-controlled, open-access dataset (Figure 2). Detailed descriptions of each component of the framework are provided below. Definitions of key taxonomic concept alignment terms are provided in Supplementary 1.</span></p><p class="c3"><span style="overflow: hidden; display: inline-block; margin: 0.00px 0.00px; border: 0.00px solid #000000; transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); -webkit-transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); width: 624.00px; height: 351.09px;"><img alt="" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," style="width: 624.00px; height: 351.09px; margin-left: -0.00px; margin-top: -0.00px; transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); -webkit-transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px);" title=""></span></p><p class="c26 c14"><span class="c41 c15">Figure 2. Workflow of the Bat Taxonomic Alignment</span><sup><a href="#cmnt14" id="cmnt_ref14">[n]</a></sup><span class="c7">. The BTA integrates taxonomic treatments from historical and contemporary authorities, reconciles taxonomic concepts through a combination of automated matching and expert review, and disseminates the resulting concept-alignment framework as a versioned, open-access resource. Historical and legacy authorities were digitized and standardized alongside contemporary digital taxonomies, enabling taxonomic concepts to be linked across 45 years of bat systematics. The resulting framework preserves provenance, documents concept relationships, and supports FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable) dissemination through versioned releases on Zenodo.</span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c33 c32"></span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c33 c32">Taxonomic scope and authority coverage</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">The Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA) was developed as a proof-of-concept framework for documenting and reconciling taxonomic concepts across changing classifications and through time. Bats provide an ideal system in which to develop and test such a framework because they have experienced extensive taxonomic revision over recent decades, resulting in substantial changes to species circumscriptions across major taxonomic authorities. </span><span class="c9">Because bats underpin research and decision-making across fields ranging from conservation and biodiversity monitoring to disease ecology and public health, these changes can have far-reaching consequences. </span><span class="c9">Although </span><span class="c9">BTA </span><span class="c9">is</span><span class="c7">&nbsp;developed using bats as a model system, the underlying challenge of reconciling changing taxonomic concepts extends throughout biodiversity science.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">To capture this dynamic history, the BTA incorporates taxonomic treatments from </span><span class="c9">fifteen </span><span class="c9">major authorities</span><span class="c7">&nbsp;spanning 45 years (1980&ndash;2025; Table 1). Authorities were selected to represent major milestones in the development of bat taxonomy, including globally recognized taxonomic references, conservation taxonomies, and contemporary digital taxonomic resources. These include successive editions of Mammal Species of the World (MSW), Handbook of the Mammals of the World (HMW), BatNames, the Mammal Diversity Database (MDD), and the IUCN Red List, together with additional authorities that have played important roles in shaping contemporary bat taxonomy. Collectively, these works provide a chronological record of changing species circumscriptions, synonymies, genus assignments, and higher-level classifications, documenting a period during which recognized bat diversity increased from fewer than 1,000 species in the early 1980s to 1,498 species in 2025.</span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c14 c26"><span class="c41">Table 1: Taxonomic authorities incorporated into the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA). </span><span class="c7">Chronological overview of the fifteen taxonomic authorities incorporated into the BTA, spanning 1980&ndash;2025. Together, these resources document the transition from historical printed classifications to contemporary versioned digital taxonomies and capture a period during which recognized bat diversity increased from 950 to 1,498 species.</span></p><p class="c26 c14"><span class="c7">&nbsp;</span></p><table class="c37"><tr class="c5"><td class="c53" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">Taxonomic Authority </span></p></td><td class="c46" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">Format</span></p></td><td class="c62" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">Acronym</span></p></td><td class="c65" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">Year Published</span></p></td><td class="c56" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">Valid Species Count</span></p></td><td class="c50" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c68 c69">Citation</span><sup><a href="#cmnt15" id="cmnt_ref15">[o]</a></sup></p></td><td class="c49" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c2"></span></p></td><td class="c49" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c2"></span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c55" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">A World List of Mammalian Species</span></p></td><td class="c58" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">printed</span></p></td><td class="c66" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">CH1_1980</span></p></td><td class="c28" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">1980</span></p></td><td class="c52" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">950</span></p></td><td class="c44" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Corbet and Hill 1980</span></p></td><td class="c0" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c10"></span></p></td><td class="c0" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 