 class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Batnames.org Species List Version 1.4</span></p></td><td class="c19" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">digital</span></p></td><td class="c29" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">BatNames_2023</span></p></td><td class="c20" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">2023</span></p></td><td class="c6" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c68">1469</span></p></td><td class="c23" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Simmons and Cirranello 2023</span></p></td><td class="c24" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c10"></span></p></td><td class="c24" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c10"></span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c16" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Mammal Diversity Database Version 1.11</span></p></td><td class="c19" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">digital</span></p></td><td class="c29" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">MDD_2023</span></p></td><td class="c20" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">2023</span></p></td><td class="c6" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">1468</span></p></td><td class="c23" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Mammal Diversity Database 2023</span></p></td><td class="c24" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c10"></span></p></td><td class="c24" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c10"></span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c16" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Mammal Diversity Database Version 2.0</span></p></td><td class="c19" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">digital</span></p></td><td class="c29" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">MDD_2025_2.0</span></p></td><td class="c20" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">2025</span></p></td><td class="c6" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">1487</span></p></td><td class="c23" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Mammal Diversity Database 2025a</span></p></td><td class="c24" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c10"></span></p></td><td class="c24" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c10"></span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c16" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Batnames.org Species List Version 1.7</span></p></td><td class="c19" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">digital</span></p></td><td class="c29" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">batnames_2025_1.7</span></p></td><td class="c20" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">2025</span></p></td><td class="c6" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">1487</span></p></td><td class="c23" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Simmons and Cirranello 2025</span></p></td><td class="c24" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c10"></span></p></td><td class="c24" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c10"></span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c16" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Mammal Diversity Database Version 2.2</span></p></td><td class="c19" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">digital</span></p></td><td class="c29" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">MDD_2025_2.2</span></p></td><td class="c20" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">2025</span></p></td><td class="c6" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">1498</span></p></td><td class="c23" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Mammal Diversity Database 2025b</span></p></td><td class="c24" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c10"></span></p></td><td class="c24" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14 c8"><span class="c10"></span></p></td></tr></table><p class="c3"><span class="c9">The resulting framework preserves taxonomic treatments across more than four decades of bat systematics while retaining the original context</span><span class="c7">&nbsp;and provenance associated with each authority. By documenting how species concepts are treated across successive classifications, the BTA provides a foundation for tracking taxonomic change, identifying areas of agreement and disagreement among authorities, and linking historical and contemporary biodiversity data. Detailed descriptions of data acquisition and digitization workflows, dataset standardization procedures, and provenance preservation are provided in Supplementary 2. </span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c33 c32">Concept alignment framework</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">Taxonomic concepts were aligned across successive classifications using a combination of automated matching and manual expert review. The objective was not simply to reconcile name strings, but to determine how species concepts were treated across authorities and through time. </span><span class="c9">The BTA aligns taxonomic concepts across successive authorities by documenting how species treatments change through time. </span><span class="c9">Rather than recording only accepted names, the framework captures the broader taxonomic context associated with each concept, including subspecies, synonyms, alternative names, taxonomic notes, </span><span class="c7">and supporting treatments. This information allows concepts to be traced across classifications even when names, circumscriptions, or taxonomic interpretations change.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">Taxonomic concepts are linked using a standardized set of documented relationships that capture common forms of taxonomic change (Figure 3). These include species splits, species mergers, transfers, spelling changes and nomina dubia. Newly described species represent taxa formally recognized as new to science, whereas species splits and mergers reflect changes in species circumscriptions. Transfers record movement of taxa within the taxonomic hierarchy, typically between genera or higher-level groups. Spelling changes capture orthographic corrections and nomenclatural standardizations, while nomina dubia represent names of uncertain application. Together, these relationships provide a consistent framework for documenting how taxonomic concepts evolve through time.</span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c9 c63"></span></p><p class="c4 c14"><span style="overflow: hidden; display: inline-block; margin: 0.00px 0.00px; border: 0.00px solid #000000; transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); -webkit-transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); width: 624.00px; height: 94.67px;"><img alt="" src="data:image/png;base64," style="width: 624.00px; height: 94.67px; margin-left: -0.00px; margin-top: -0.00px; transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); -webkit-transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px);" title=""></span></p><p class="c40 c14"><span class="c41">Figure 3. Taxonomic concept relationships recorded within the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA).</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;The BTA documents how species concepts change across taxonomic authorities and through time using a standardized set of concept relationships. Illustrated examples include newly described species, species splits, species mergers, transfers, </span><span class="c9">spelling </span><sup><a href="#cmnt18" id="cmnt_ref18">[r]</a></sup><span class="c7">changes, and nomina dubia. By explicitly recording these relationships, the framework links historical and contemporary taxonomic treatments while preserving provenance and the context associated with taxonomic decisions. Bat silhouettes were obtained from PhyloPic.</span></p><p class="c40 c14 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">A key feature of the BTA is the preservation of provenance. Rather than replacing historical classifications with a single preferred taxonomy, the framework retains authority-specific treatments together with their associated taxonomic notes, source citations, and supporting information. Complete verbatim treatments are preserved within the dataset, enabling users to examine the original context in which </span><span class="c9">taxonomic </span><span class="c7">decisions were made and to trace the evidence supporting changes in species circumscriptions through time. This allows users to determine, for example, when a species concept was first recognized, which historical concepts contributed to a contemporary species, how different authorities interpreted species limits, and what taxonomic evidence or geographic scope underpinned those decisions. By maintaining direct links to the original treatments, the BTA provides a transparent record of taxonomic reasoning rather than only the final taxonomic outcome.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">The resulting framework functions as an alignment layer among taxonomic authorities rather than a replacement taxonomy. By documenting concept relationships, preserving provenance, and retaining alternative names and treatments, the BTA enables biodiversity data collected under different classifications to be interpreted within an explicit taxonomic context. Detailed descriptions of concept matching procedures, relationship definitions, and manual reconciliation examples are provided in Supplementary 3.</span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c33 c32">Resource format and accessibility</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">The BTA is distributed as a version-controlled, open-access resource on Zenodo (Sherman et al., 2025) in both human-readable and machine-readable formats. </span><span class="c9 c15">It can be accessed at: </span><span class="c9 c15">XXX</span><span class="c9">. Each </span><span class="c7">release receives a persistent DOI, enabling users to cite the exact version used in analyses while ensuring that previous versions remain permanently accessible.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">The BTA is a spreadsheet organized around species concepts and consists of four primary components: (i) Valid Names, an interpreted index of species concepts across authorities; (ii) Full Treatments, which preserve verbatim authority-specific taxonomic accounts and supporting notes; (iii) Alternative Names, a comprehensive index of synonyms, subspecies, misspellings, abbreviations, and historical name variants; and (iv) concept relationship tables documenting how species concepts change across authorities and through time. An overview of the column layout and contents is summarized in Table 2.</span></p><hr style="page-break-before:always;display:none;"><p class="c25 c14 c8"><span class="c7 c15"></span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c41">Table 2. Overview of the principal components and fields contained within the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA).</span><span class="c7">&nbsp;This table describes the structure of the BTA and the fields used to record taxonomic concepts, authority-specific treatments, concept relationships, provenance, and alternative names.</span></p><table class="c37"><tr class="c5"><td class="c36" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">BTA Columns</span></p></td><td class="c64" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">Brief explanation of column contents</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c67" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">A-O </span></p></td><td class="c39" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">valid names according to each of the fifteen taxonomic authorities</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">Q </span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Taxonomic Notes (Concatenated) - Consolidated taxonomic notes extracted from source treatments, including supporting rationale, citations, and authority identifiers (e.g. [HMW]) to preserve provenance and facilitate rapid interpretation of taxonomic decisions.</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c61"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">S-AL</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Subspecies and Synonyms &ndash; Subspecies and synonyms reported by the source authority. Subspecies are shown in bold and synonyms in italics following MSW conventions; names following a bolded subspecies indicate synonyms associated with that subspecies.</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">AN-AQ</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Corbet and Hill 1980)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">AH-AY</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Honacki et al., 1982)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">AZ-BC</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Corbet and Hill 1991)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">BD-BK</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Koopman 1993)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">BL-CC</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Koopman 1994)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">CD-DA</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Wilson &amp; Reeder 2005)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">DB-EA</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Wilson &amp; Mittermeier 2019)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">EB-ES</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Simmons and Cirranello 2022)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">ET-GM</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Mammal Diversity Database 2022)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">GN-HV</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (IUCN 2022)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">HW-IM</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Simmons and Cirranello 2023)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">IN-KK</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Mammal Diversity Database 2023)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">KL-MJ</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Mammal Diversity Database 2025a)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">MK-NC</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Simmons and Cirranello 2025)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">ND-PD</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Full verbatim species treatment (Mammal Diversity Database 2025b)</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">PE</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Concatenated list of all alternative names</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c5"><td class="c13" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c2">PF-TF</span></p></td><td class="c17" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c12 c14"><span class="c10">Agreement Index</span></p></td></tr></table><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">Together, these components enable users to track taxonomic concepts across 45 years of bat systematics, trace the provenance of taxonomic decisions, identify alternative treatments, and reconcile biodiversity data collected under different classifications. The resource is accompanied by documentation, version histories, and a video walkthrough, all hosted on Zenodo, to facilitate reuse across biodiversity informatics, conservation, ecological research, and related applications.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">To maximize interoperability, the BTA follows FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable; Wilkinson et al., 2016) data principles and is designed as a living resource that can be updated as new taxonomic evidence emerges and additional authorities become available.</span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><hr style="page-break-before:always;display:none;"><p class="c25 c14 c8"><span class="c18 c9"></span></p><h1 class="c1"><span class="c18 c9">Applications of the BTA</span></h1><p class="c3"><span class="c7">A primary application of the BTA is the reconciliation of biodiversity data collected under different taxonomic classifications. By linking taxonomic concepts across successive authorities, the framework enables users to determine how a species was treated at a particular point in time and how that treatment relates to current taxonomy. This allows historical records, publications, museum collections, conservation assessments, field guides, and biodiversity databases to be interpreted within their original taxonomic context while remaining connected to contemporary classifications. </span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">To illustrate these capabilities, we present two applications of the framework. First, we quantify patterns of disagreement among major bat taxonomic authorities spanning 45 years of taxonomic revision. Second, we use the framework to audit taxonomic consistency within the IUCN Red List, demonstrating how discrepancies among taxonomic authorities can propagate into biodiversity and conservation infrastructure.</span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c33 c32">Example application 1: Quantifying disagreement among taxonomic authorities</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">The BTA </span><span class="c9 c15">v</span><span class="c9 c15">XX</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;was used to align 1,680 epithets and </span><span class="c9">2,185 binomial combinations</span><sup><a href="#cmnt19" id="cmnt_ref19">[s]</a></sup><span class="c9">&nbsp;across </span><span class="c9">fifteen </span><span class="c7">taxonomic authorities between 1980 and 2025, creating a chronological record of changing species concepts and taxonomic treatments in Chiroptera.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">To quantify agreement among authorities, we calculated an Agreement Index based on pairwise comparisons of aligned taxonomic concepts. For each epithet, authorities were scored as either concordant (0) or discordant (1) in their treatment of the taxon. Disagreement was recorded when authorities differed in their interpretation of a concept, for example when one authority recognized a taxon as a valid species while another treated it as a synonym, subspecies, or alternative taxonomic concept. Summing these pairwise differences provided a measure of taxonomic incongruence among authorities over time, which we visualized as a heatmap (Figure 4).</span></p><p class="c40 c14"><span style="overflow: hidden; display: inline-block; margin: 0.00px 0.00px; border: 0.00px solid #000000; transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); -webkit-transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); width: 624.00px; height: 564.00px;"><img alt="" src="data:image/png;base64," style="width: 624.00px; height: 564.00px; margin-left: 0.00px; margin-top: 0.00px; transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); -webkit-transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px);" title=""></span></p><p class="c40 c14"><span class="c41">Figure 4. Taxonomic disagreement among 15 bat taxonomic authorities spanning 1980&ndash;2025.</span><span class="c7">&nbsp;Values indicate the number of aligned taxonomic concepts receiving different treatments between pairs of authorities. Higher values indicate greater disagreement in species circumscriptions, synonymies, or taxonomic placement. The heatmap reveals decreasing divergence among recent authorities, but persistent disagreement between the IUCN Red List and contemporary taxonomic authorities, motivating the audit presented in Application 2.</span></p><p class="c40 c14 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">Although many species concepts were treated consistently across authorities, substantial disagreement was observed among several modern classifications. For example, 349 disagreements were identified between HMW (2019) and MDD v.2.2 (2025), reflecting extensive recent taxonomic revision. Comparisons involving the IUCN Red List revealed 443 disagreements with BatNames 2025 and 451 disagreements with MDD v.2.2 (2025), highlighting the challenges of maintaining alignment among rapidly evolving taxonomic resources. The BTA identified numerous cases in which identical or similar name strings were associated with different taxonomic concepts across authorities. For example, the IUCN Red List recognizes </span><span class="c9 c42">Lasiurus salinae</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;as a valid species, whereas HMW treats the taxon as </span><span class="c9 c42">Lasiurus blossevillii salinae</span><span class="c7">, reflecting a subspecies-level interpretation. Although the underlying epithet is shared, the taxonomic concepts represented by these treatments differ.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">Together, these results demonstrate that while broad agreement exists across much of bat taxonomy, important areas of disagreement persist among major authorities. By explicitly aligning concepts rather than names, the BTA makes these discrepancies visible and traceable through time.</span></p><p class="c25 c14 c8"><span class="c32 c33"></span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c33 c32">Example application 2: Auditing taxonomic consistency within conservation infrastructure</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">The IUCN Red List is among the most widely used biodiversity resources globally and serves as the taxonomic foundation for many conservation assessments, policy decisions, funding mechanisms, and biodiversity databases (</span><span class="c9 c15">CITE CITE</span><span class="c9">). Because taxonomic concepts continue to change through time, maintaining alignment between conservation infrastructure and contemporary taxonomic authorities represents an ongoing challenge (</span><span class="c9 c15">CITE CITE</span><span class="c7">).</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">To demonstrate the utility of the BTA for taxonomic auditing, in September 2024 we audited bat taxonomy within the IUCN Red List by comparing species concepts recognized by the IUCN Red List with those recognized by the </span><span class="c9">Mammal Diversity Database (MDD) v2.2 (2025),</span><sup><a href="#cmnt20" id="cmnt_ref20">[t]</a></sup><sup><a href="#cmnt21" id="cmnt_ref21">[u]</a></sup><span class="c9">&nbsp;the most recent global mammal taxonomic authority available at the time of the audit. </span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">This </span><span class="c9">comparison </span><span class="c9">identified 287</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;taxonomic </span><sup><a href="#cmnt22" id="cmnt_ref22">[v]</a></sup><sup><a href="#cmnt23" id="cmnt_ref23">[w]</a></sup><span class="c9">modifications </span><span class="c9">affecting bat species within the IUCN Red List, representing ~</span><span class="c9">20% of assessed bat species</span><sup><a href="#cmnt24" id="cmnt_ref24">[x]</a></sup><sup><a href="#cmnt25" id="cmnt_ref25">[y]</a></sup><span class="c9">.</span><sup><a href="#cmnt26" id="cmnt_ref26">[z]</a></sup><span class="c7">&nbsp;The discrepancies spanned multiple categories of taxonomic change, including species splits, newly recognized species absent from IUCN records, generic reassignments, spelling corrections, species mergers, and rank changes (Figure 5).</span></p><p class="c3"><span style="overflow: hidden; display: inline-block; margin: 0.00px 0.00px; border: 0.00px solid #000000; transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); -webkit-transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); width: 630.16px; height: 275.63px;"><img alt="" src="data:image/png;base64," style="width: 630.16px; height: 275.63px; margin-left: -0.00px; margin-top: -0.00px; transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px); -webkit-transform: rotate(0.00rad) translateZ(0px);" title=""></span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c41">Figure 5. Categories of taxonomic modifications identified during an audit of bat taxonomy within the IUCN Red List.</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;Comparison of IUCN Red List taxonomic treatments with Mammal Diversity Database (MDD) </span><span class="c9">v2.2 (2025)</span><span class="c7">&nbsp;identified 287 modifications. These discrepancies illustrate how differences among taxonomic authorities can accumulate within downstream conservation infrastructure.</span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">One illustrative example involves the </span><span class="c9 c42">Myotis nattereri</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;species complex. While recent authorities such as BatNames and the Mammal Diversity Database recognize several distinct species within this complex, including </span><span class="c9 c42">Myotis hoveli</span><span class="c9">, </span><span class="c9 c42">M. crypticus</span><span class="c9">, </span><span class="c9 c42">M. escalerai</span><span class="c9">, and </span><span class="c9 c42">M. zenatius </span><span class="c7">(e.g., &Ccedil;oraman et al., 2019), the IUCN Red List continues to retain a broader historical circumscription. Such differences alter the biological entities being assessed and may influence interpretations of geographic range size, conservation status, and management priorities.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">The resulting audit was compiled into a detailed report and submitted to the IUCN bat specialist community for review. In addition to identifying discrepancies, the report provided supporting taxonomic notes, concept relationships, literature references, provenance information, and recommended actions for each affected record. By linking historical and contemporary species concepts within a transparent framework, the BTA enables large-scale taxonomic audits that would otherwise require extensive manual reconciliation. This application demonstrates how concept-level taxonomic alignment can be used not only to compare taxonomic authorities but also to evaluate the consistency of biodiversity infrastructure that depends upon them.</span></p><h1 class="c1"><span class="c9 c18">Discussion</span></h1><p class="c3"><span class="c9">We developed the Bat Taxonomic Alignment to reconcile taxonomic concepts across </span><span class="c9">fifteen </span><span class="c7">authorities spanning 45 years of bat systematics and to evaluate how changing taxonomies influence biodiversity data through time. Our analyses demonstrate that agreement among taxonomic authorities cannot be assumed, even within a comparatively well-studied mammalian order. Although scientific names are often treated as stable identifiers, the BTA revealed substantial differences in how authorities circumscribe species concepts, assign synonymies, and recognize taxonomic boundaries, with hundreds of conflicting species treatments persisting among contemporary classifications despite increasing efforts toward taxonomic standardization. Application of the framework to the IUCN Red List further identified 287 taxonomic modifications affecting ~20% of assessed bat species, demonstrating how differences among taxonomic authorities can propagate into biodiversity infrastructure. Together, these results highlight a fundamental challenge facing modern biodiversity science: scientific names are frequently used as proxies for biological entities, yet the taxonomic concepts they represent can change substantially through time. By explicitly aligning taxonomic concepts rather than names alone, the BTA makes these changes visible, traceable, and reproducible.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">Although demonstrated here using bats, the challenges identified by this study are not unique to Chiroptera. Similar disagreements among competing taxonomic authorities occur across many groups of organisms, including birds, reptiles, amphibians, plants, fungi, insects, and microbial taxa, where alternative classifications frequently coexist and species circumscriptions continue to evolve (</span><span class="c9 c15">ADD CITATIONS</span><span class="c7">). Anyone working with biodiversity information across long time periods - including researchers, conservation organizations, government agencies, and consultants - must therefore contend with the fact that a scientific name may refer to different biological entities depending on the authority consulted and the date at which a classification was adopted. The resulting ambiguity complicates efforts to integrate biodiversity information across datasets, institutions, and time periods, particularly when historical records are interpreted using contemporary taxonomic frameworks.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">Our IUCN audit illustrates how these discrepancies can propagate into downstream biodiversity infrastructure. As shown by the modifications identified between the IUCN Red List and MDD v2.2 (2025), taxonomic divergence is not restricted to specialist taxonomic resources but can persist within systems that underpin conservation planning, policy development, funding allocation, and biodiversity monitoring (</span><span class="c9 c15">CITE CITE</span><span class="c9">). Many discrepancies reflected differences in species circumscriptions, including species splits, newly recognized taxa, and alternative interpretations of species limits, thereby altering the biological entities being assessed and potentially influencing estimates of geographic range size, population status, extinction risk, and conservation priority (</span><span class="c9 c15">CITE CITE</span><span class="c7">).</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">The patterns identified in the IUCN audit closely mirror the conceptual framework presented in Figure 1. When a historical species concept is subsequently divided into multiple descendant taxa, biodiversity records, conservation assessments, ecological observations, and other downstream datasets may remain linked to the historical name even though the biological entity represented by that name has changed</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;</span><span class="c9">. Such discrepancies are not merely nomenclatural; they alter the units through which biodiversity is measured, monitored, and managed. The implications extend well beyond conservation assessments (</span><span class="c9">McNeely 2002; Schlick-Steiner et al. 2010; Wu et al. 2019; Almeida-Silva et al. 2021</span><sup><a href="#cmnt27" id="cmnt_ref27">[aa]</a></sup><span class="c9">). Ecological niche models, species interaction networks, phylogenetic analyses, genomic repositories, and host&ndash;pathogen studies all depend upon accurate taxonomic attribution (Kennedy et al., 2005; Radar et al., 2023). The </span><span class="c9 c42">Miniopterus schreibersii</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;species complex illustrates this challenge because taxonomic revision substantially altered the geographic distribution represented by the species name (</span><span class="c9">Tian et al. 2004</span><sup><a href="#cmnt28" id="cmnt_ref28">[ab]</a></sup><span class="c7">), meaning that historical pathogen records associated with the broader concept (e.g. Zheng et al., 2018) may not necessarily refer to the same biological entity recognized today. Without explicit concept alignment, such changes can obscure host distributions, affect interpretations of disease risk, and complicate comparisons among studies conducted under different taxonomic frameworks (Tsang et al., 2016).</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">The discrepancies observed here also highlight broader challenges facing contemporary taxonomic infrastructure. First, many biodiversity resources continue to operate without explicit versioning, despite its importance for retaining provenance links among taxonomic concepts, sources, and rationales (Karbstein et al. 2024). Although several initiatives have adopted version-controlled approaches, including the Mammal Diversity Database (Burgin et al., 2025), ZooBank (Pyle, 2016), Plazi (Agosti et al., 2022), and the Catalogue of Life (Garnett et al., 2020), many datasets and publications do not clearly indicate which taxonomic treatment underlies a record or analysis. The IUCN audit provides a practical example of this challenge. While updates are incorporated through Taxonomic Change Requests and expert review processes, the taxonomic backbone associated with particular assessments is not always explicitly versioned or easily reconstructed retrospectively. There are often substantial delays between the publication of taxonomic revisions and their incorporation into downstream biodiversity resources. During these intervals, newly recognized taxa may be absent from major databases or aggregated under broader taxonomic concepts, potentially obscuring biodiversity patterns and limiting discoverability in data-driven analyses (Garnett &amp; Christidis, 2017; Troudet et al., 2018; GBIF Secretariat, 2025). Versioning is therefore essential not only for reproducibility, but also for documenting how taxonomic concepts change and propagate through biodiversity infrastructure.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">Second, the provenance of taxonomic decisions is often difficult to trace. Many authoritative classifications are maintained by relatively small expert communities operating under substantial resource constraints that limit data sharing and transparency (Bruneman et al., 2008; Garnett, 2020; Pyle, 2016; Agosti, 2022; Ebach et al., 2011). Expert oversight remains indispensable, yet the rationale underlying taxonomic changes is not always archived in a manner that can be readily consulted by downstream users. Researchers frequently encounter updated classifications without access to the discussions, evidence, alternative treatments, or historical context that informed those decisions. Consequently, disagreements among authorities may be visible, but the reasons underlying those disagreements can remain difficult to evaluate. By preserving authority-specific treatments, taxonomic notes, literature references, and concept relationships, concept-alignment tools such as the BTA attempt to make this provenance more transparent and accessible.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">Third, access to taxonomic knowledge remains uneven. Important taxonomic references may be difficult to obtain, unavailable in machine-readable formats, or inaccessible because of financial, linguistic, geographic, or institutional barriers (</span><span class="c9 c15">CITE CITE</span><span class="c9">&nbsp;; Karbstein et al. 2024; Cornelius et al., 2025). The incorporation of historical authorities into the BTA required substantial digitization and semantic annotation efforts, highlighting the broader challenge of mobilizing legacy taxonomic knowledge for contemporary biodiversity science. The digitization of Handbook of the Mammals of the World treatments provides one example of the effort required to transform foundational taxonomic resources into reusable digital knowledge. Similar barriers continue to limit access to the taxonomic information needed to interpret biodiversity data consistently across regions and disciplines (</span><span class="c9 c15">CITE CITE</span><span class="c7">).</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c9">Collectively, these challenges suggest that biodiversity infrastructure is increasingly constrained by workflows built around static taxonomic lists. As taxonomic discovery accelerates through the integration of molecular, morphological, ecological, and biogeographic evidence, maintaining consistency across databases, conservation assessments, museum collections, and analytical pipelines requires mechanisms capable of accommodating continual revision while preserving historical context. Taxonomic updates are most useful when they are versioned, citable, machine-readable, and accompanied by sufficient detail to enable downstream users to understand and reproduce taxonomic decisions. One potential path forward is the adoption of more formalized and regularly published taxonomic update workflows, similar to those used by initiatives such as the Catalogue of Fishes (</span><span class="c9 c15">CITE</span><span class="c9">), the IOC World Bird List (</span><span class="c9 c15">CITE</span><span class="c9">), and the International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses (</span><span class="c9 c15">CITE</span><span class="c7">) where changes are documented, archived, and disseminated through versioned releases. Such approaches improve transparency, facilitate interoperability among biodiversity resources, and provide a stable foundation for tracking taxonomic concepts through time.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">The BTA represents one approach to addressing these challenges, serving as an alignment layer among taxonomic authorities. By organizing authority-specific treatments into a structured, versioned framework, the BTA enables users to reconcile historical and contemporary classifications without losing the context in which records were originally generated. Users can determine how a species was treated by a particular authority, identify alternative names associated with a concept, trace taxonomic changes through time, and translate records collected under older classifications into contemporary taxonomic frameworks. These capabilities are particularly relevant for historical literature, museum collections, biodiversity databases, field guides, and conservation assessments that continue to rely upon classifications that may no longer reflect current understanding.</span></p><p class="c3"><span class="c7">More broadly, the principles underlying the BTA - concept alignment, provenance preservation, versioning, and interoperability - are applicable across biodiversity science and other disciplines that rely on evolving classification systems. As biological disciplines increasingly rely upon large, interconnected datasets, taxonomic concepts must become as traceable and interoperable as occurrence records, genomic sequences, ecological observations, and conservation assessments. The challenge addressed here is therefore not fundamentally about bat taxonomy, but about ensuring that biological knowledge remains interpretable as our understanding of biodiversity continues to change. By documenting relationships among taxonomic concepts across 45 years of bat systematics, the BTA provides a practical framework for reconciling changing classifications while preserving provenance, ultimately supporting more reproducible biodiversity science, conservation assessment, and data integration.</span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"></span></p><p class="c3 c8"><span class="c7"><br></span></p><p class="c4 c8 c38"><span class="c7 c21"></span></p><p class="c4 c8 c38"><span class="c7 c21"></span></p><p class="c4 c8 c38"><span class="c7 c21"></span></p><p class="c4 c38"><span class="c9 c21">References</span><sup><a href="#cmnt29" id="cmnt_ref29">[ac]</a></sup></p><div><p class="c14 c8 c45"><span class="c11"></span></p></div><div class="c34"><p class="c12"><a href="#cmnt_ref1" id="cmnt1">[a]</a><span class="c11">genomics can be used for evolutionary, conservation, epidemiology, etc, I see it more as a tool</span></p></div><div class="c34"><p class="c12"><a href="#cmnt_ref2" id="cmnt2">[b]</a><span class="c11">we are not really revising species concepts in the BTA. but now I have the pressing question, what species concept is the BTA using? In case of taxonomic disagreements, what does the BTA decide?</span></p></div><div class="c34"><p class="c12"><a href="#cmnt_ref3" id="cmnt3">[c]</a><span class="c11">do any of these species change IUCN category? E.g. from&nbsp;Endangered to Critically Endangered, or somethis similar?</span></p></div><div class="c34"><p class="c12"><a href="#cmnt_ref4" id="cmnt4">[d]</a><span class="c11">Note for Ariadna: check if the text below specifies how many of these are &quot;Not Evaluated, Data Deficient, Least Concern, Near Threatened, Vulnerable, Endangered, Critically Endangered, Extinct in the Wild and Extinct&quot;</span></p></div><div class="c34"><p class="c12"><a href="#cmnt_ref5" id="cmnt5">[e]</a><span class="c11">can we cite an example? Would this one fit?&nbsp;https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-46979-1</span></p></div><div class="c34"><p class="c12"><a href="#cmnt_ref6" id="cmnt6">[f]</a><span class="c11">Bayraktarov, Elisa, Glenn Ehmke, James O&#39;Connor, Emma L. Burns, Hoang A. Nguyen, Louise McRae, Hugh P. Possingham, and David B. Lindenmayer. &quot;Do big unstructured biodiversity data mean more knowledge?.&quot; Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution 6 (2019): 239.</span></p></div><div class="c34"><p class="c12"><a href="#cmnt_ref7" id="cmnt7">[g]</a><span class="c11">Heberling, J. Mason, Joseph T. Miller, Daniel Noesgaard, Scott B. Weingart, and Dmitry Schigel. &quot;Data integration enables global biodiversity synthesis.&quot; Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 118, no. 6 (2021): e2018093118.</span></p></div><div class="c34"><p class="c12"><a href="#cmnt_ref8" id="cmnt8">[h]</a><span class="c11">define or give example</span></p></div><div class="c34"><p class="c12"><a href="#cmnt_ref9" id="cmnt9">[i]</a><span class="c11">Zachos, Frank E. &quot;(New) Species concepts, species delimitation and the inherent limitations of taxonomy.&quot; Jour